  "query_preview": "..\nThrough next Saturday...\n\nCold front moving thru the region early this morning will bring a\nshift to breezy NW-N winds. Along with a very poor humidity\nrecovery this will keep fire weather conditions critical. By\nsunrise the strongest winds will be in the east, and by midday\nthose winds will begin to relax, while western lower elevations \nsee a shift to lighter easterly winds. The higher elevations will\ncontinue to see gusty SW-W winds through the day. A Red Flag\nWarning remains in effect so please take care not to spark a fire.\n\nSaturday saw very active wildfires across the PacNW and we are now\nstarting to see reduced surface visibilities upstream in the \npost-frontal subsidence. Still not quite sure how much of an\nimpact on air quality this will have, but it is something to\nmonitor as there will almost certainly be some magnitude of\nincreased surface smoke in our forecast area today. A stronger\nfrontal passage tonight is expected to bring another push of\nsurface smoke late tonight into Monday. \n\nLook for temps today about 10F cooler than Saturday. Next dose of\ncold advection commences by this evening bringing breezy north\nwinds (gusts 25-35 mph), and in fact there will be another push of\ncold advection from the north by daybreak Monday (associated with\nthe main upper level trof). There may be a few light showers\nSunday night into early Monday along a mid level baroclinic zone,\nbut otherwise the main impact will be the much cooler air\n(FINALLY) for Monday & Tuesday. Look for highs in the 70s to lower\n80s on these days, with much cooler nights, as a ~1020mb surface\nhigh settles in. Smoke conditions early next week are tough to\ndetermine. There should be enough northerly low level flow to\nimprove the surface smoke by late Monday, but feel that lofted \nsmoke will continue to produce hazy skies due to the abundance of \nactive fires to our west.\n\nEnsemble consensus is for rising heights with a transition to\nflat ridging during the latter half of the week. As previous \ndiscussions have mentioned, there may be periodic weak glancing \nshortwaves associated doses of modest cool advection, but the \noverwhelming consensus is for temps warming back to the 80s \nWednesday and lower-mid 90s Thursday to Saturday. The flat flow \nshould keep things dry through the period. By late weekend into \nthe following week, we may see a return to monsoonal flow which \nwould raise pwats and introduce thunderstorm potential once again.\n\nJKL",
